From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude Agent <claude-agent@invalid>
Date: Mon, 12 Oct 2026 06:53:17 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] tests/color-hdr: Bound the channel residue of single
 precision transforms
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The channel bound compared a channel that is 0 in the input with a fixed
allowance of 1e-4 cd/m². Both conversions run in single precision with
their matrices combined into one, gamut to linear BT.2020, the white
adaptation, and back, so such a channel comes out as the rounding residue
of the other channels: about one unit in the last place of the brightest
input channel, of either sign. Linear 0.5 is 5000 cd/m², so one such unit
is about 6e-4 cd/m², six times the allowance. The DCI-P3 output showed
4.03e-4 cd/m² where the unadapted conversion's residue was negative and
clamped to 0.

Allow 8 FLT_EPSILON of the brightest input channel in absolute luminance,
4.8e-3 cd/m² for linear 0.5, in both comparisons, and nothing else. The
relative bounds are unchanged. A white-normalized Bradford adaptation, which
the bound exists to reject, brightens a channel by about 0.5 %, some
24 cd/m² there.
---
 src/tests/clutter/conform/color-hdr.c | 20 +++++++++++++++++---
 1 file changed, 17 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)

diff --git a/src/tests/clutter/conform/color-hdr.c b/src/tests/clutter/conform/color-hdr.c
index e94691b..1984eed 100644
--- a/src/tests/clutter/conform/color-hdr.c
+++ b/src/tests/clutter/conform/color-hdr.c
@@ -3737,6 +3737,16 @@ white_adaptation_continuity (void)
  * normalization of a Bradford adaptation brightens, and white, at every
  * temperature from 1000 K to 10000 K, on a BT.2020 and a DCI-P3 output. Each
  * channel is exactly the unadapted channel scaled by its factor.
+ *
+ * Both conversions run in single precision with their matrices combined
+ * into one, so a channel that is 0 in the input comes out as the rounding
+ * residue of the other channels, adapted or not: about one unit in the last
+ * place of the brightest input channel, of either sign, which the encoding
+ * clamps at 0. Beyond the relative bound, both comparisons allow 8
+ * FLT_EPSILON of the brightest input channel in absolute luminance and
+ * nothing else: 4.8e-3 cd/m² for a linear 0.5, which is 5000 cd/m². A
+ * white-normalized Bradford adaptation brightens a channel by about 0.5 %,
+ * some 24 cd/m² there, and its cross-talk lights channels that are 0.
  */
 static void
 white_adaptation_channel_bound (void)
@@ -3784,16 +3794,20 @@ white_adaptation_channel_bound (void)
 
           for (k = 0; k < G_N_ELEMENTS (inputs); k++)
             {
-              double lum[3], plain_lum[3];
+              double lum[3], plain_lum[3], residue;
 
+              /* Linear 1.0 is the PQ peak, 10000 cd/m². */
+              residue = 8.0 * FLT_EPSILON * 10000.0 *
+                        MAX (inputs[k][0], MAX (inputs[k][1], inputs[k][2]));
               transform_to_luminance (blending, output, inputs[k], lum);
               transform_to_luminance (plain, output, inputs[k], plain_lum);
               for (c = 0; c < 3; c++)
                 {
-                  g_assert_cmpfloat (lum[c], <=, plain_lum[c] * (1.0 + 1e-4) + 1e-4);
+                  g_assert_cmpfloat (lum[c], <=,
+                                     plain_lum[c] * (1.0 + 1e-4) + residue);
                   g_assert_cmpfloat_with_epsilon (lum[c],
                                                   plain_lum[c] * factors[c],
-                                                  plain_lum[c] * 2e-3 + 1e-3);
+                                                  plain_lum[c] * 2e-3 + residue);
                 }
             }
         }
